+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Resource limits
===============
The rc.d script uses the `i2pd` login class from
`/etc/login.conf.d/i2pd`. Rebuild the login database only when
`/etc/login.conf.db` exists:

  # cap_mkdb /etc/login.conf

The class raises the soft and hard file-descriptor limits to 8192 and sets a
private creation mask. Ensure `kern.maxfiles` and `kern.maxfilesperproc` can
support that limit for the service account, for example in `/etc/sysctl.conf`:

  kern.maxfiles=20000
  kern.maxfilesperproc=8192

Configuration and data
======================
Router configuration is in `/etc/i2pd`; persistent keys, netDb and profiles
are in `/var/i2pd`. The packaged certificate tree is read-only under
`/usr/local/share/i2pd/certificates` by default. Keep local configuration in
`/etc/i2pd`; do not edit files under `/var/i2pd` that are managed by the
package.

Logging
=======
The rc.d service sends output to syslog and early start diagnostics through
`daemon_logger`. Use the system log and `/var/i2pd` state for diagnostics.
If you enable file logging, rotate it with `newsyslog` or send `SIGUSR1` to
the daemon after moving the file.

Graceful shutdown
=================
`rcctl stop i2pd` sends SIGINT (the rc.d template sets `rc_stop_signal=INT`)
and waits up to the 660-second `daemon_timeout` for the ordered stop and the
ten-minute transit-tunnel drain. With no transit tunnels it exits promptly. A
second SIGINT stops immediately. Do not replace this with a bare SIGTERM: that
is the prompt path and skips the drain.

TUI
===
The optional `i2pd-tui` client connects to the local UNIX socket
`/var/i2pd/i2pd-tui.sock` with mode 0600. The socket is inside a 0700 state
directory, so run the client as the service account (or configure a socket
path with matching ownership):

  # doas -u _i2pd i2pd-tui --datadir=/var/i2pd

Do not add a second service or start a second router.
